# earlyoom configuration for the address-validator host (GH #225).
#
# Install: sudo cp infra/earlyoom.default /etc/default/earlyoom \
#          && sudo systemctl restart earlyoom
# Restart, never `enable --now`: that leaves an active daemon on the arguments
# it started with. Verify: tests/unit/test_earlyoom_config.py (on the host).
#
# What it can reach here. Sessions inherit oom_score_adj -1000 from exe.dev's
# sshd, and earlyoom 1.7 skips a -1000 process exactly as the kernel does
# (kill.c:242-253), --prefer or not. So it never takes a session, VS Code
# Server or anything they launch. What it does is act before the kernel
# stalls, on this host's own processes.
#
# Why it is kept. A dry run on 2026-09-24 (stock -r 3600) chose
# wof-libpostal-s (1.9 G, badness 842), the process docs/HOST-MEMORY.md ranks
# first to lose. The next picks were the user's session bus (dbus-daemon), the
# user manager (systemd, (sd-pam)) and then postgres, fifth, which is an HTTP
# 503. This file sets the order instead of leaving it to RSS:
#
# --prefer  +300: libpostal, then SocratiCode's qdrant and ollama. All three
#           are adj 0 and restart on their own (Restart=always, or Docker's
#           unless-stopped). Matched against /proc/<pid>/comm, which the kernel
#           truncates to 15 chars, so libpostal is prefix-matched, never $-anchored.
# --avoid   -300: the service (uvicorn), postgres, and the user manager
#           (systemd, (sd-pam)), which `systemd-run --user` caps need; sshd in
#           case sessions ever stop being -1000. -300 is a shift, not an
#           exemption: postgres drops from fifth to behind every small daemon.
#           dbus-daemon is left out on purpose: dbus.socket restarts it on
#           next use, so it goes fourth, after the three preferred.
# -m 10,5   earlyoom's built-in defaults, written down: SIGTERM at 10% available,
#           SIGKILL at 5%. Debian's own file sets only -r 3600.
# -s 100,100  No effect without swap. If swap is ever added, memory alone
#           still decides, instead of earlyoom waiting for swap to drain.
#
# Debian's unit runs `/usr/bin/earlyoom $EARLYOOM_ARGS` and systemd splits that
# expansion itself: a space inside a regex makes it two arguments, and a
# backslash is dropped (\. reaches earlyoom as ., matching anything). So no
# spaces, no escapes, no inner quotes; a literal character is [(] or [.].
EARLYOOM_ARGS="-r 3600 -m 10,5 -s 100,100 --prefer ^(wof-libpostal|qdrant|ollama) --avoid ^(uvicorn|postgres|systemd|[(]sd-pam[)]|sshd)$"
